 ],
 "title": "Sulfur Metabolism in Yeast: A Complex Network of Chemicals and Genes",
 "summary": "This cluster revolves around the sulfur metabolism process in yeast, specifically in the organisms S. cerevisiae LMD17 and RM11. The genes MET17 and HSU1 play crucial roles in this process, with MET17 catalyzing the conversion of homocysteine to sulfate and HSU1 having pleiotropic roles, including the release of sulfide. The deletion of MET17 and HSU1 results in inefficient sulfide assimilation and density-dependent growth, respectively. The chemicals involved in this process include sulfate, sulfide, homocysteine, and methionine, which are interconnected through various conversions and reactions.",
